{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.ExpDeriv","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Summable","lean:module:Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.InfiniteSum.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Eval.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Degree.Definitions","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.WeierstrassFactorEstimate"],"declarations":17,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.hadamard_factorization","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.hadamard_factorization","kind":"theorem","line":332,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L332-L341","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.HasHadamardFactorization.factorization","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.HasHadamardFactorization.factorization","kind":"theorem","line":322,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L322-L322","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.HasHadamardFactorization.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.HasHadamardFactorization.mk","kind":"ctor","line":321,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L321-L321","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.HasHadamardFactorization","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.HasHadamardFactorization","kind":"class","line":301,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L301-L330","doc":"**Hadamard factorization theorem** (the stated-open core, PI-5). An entire\nfunction `f` of finite order `ρ` with `f(0) ≠ 0` factors as\n`f(z) = exp(g(z)) · ∏ₙ Eₚ(z/aₙ)`, where `(aₙ)` enumerates the zeros of `f`\n(with multiplicity), the genus `p ≤ ρ`, and `g` is a polynomial of degree\n`≤ ρ`. The canonical product converges. This is the theorem that pins the\nminimal Faddeev solution `phiBProd` among all `phiBProd·(elliptic)` multiples\n(epic qou-lbth). Mathlib v4.24.0 lacks it; the proof needs Jensen's formula,\nzero-counting, and the Weierstrass/Borel–Carathéodory growth machinery.\n\nNOTE (honest, non-vacuous): the product equation `f z = exp(g z)·∏ₙ Eₚ(z/aₙ)`\nalready forces each `aₙ` to be a zero of `f` (via `weierstrassFactor_eq_zero_iff`)\nand forces completeness of the zero enumeration (no other zeros on either\nside), so the theorem is stated in full strength, not weakened.\n\nNOTE (`0 ≤ ρ`): `IsEntireOfOrderLE` takes `ρ` as an input and is satisfiable\nat negative `ρ` (the constant `1`), where `(p : ℝ) ≤ ρ` is unsatisfiable for\n`p : ℕ`.  Without the hypothesis the statement is false —\n`hadamard_factorization_needs_nonneg_order` proves it.  The classical theorem\nhides this because the order of a nonzero entire function is `≥ 0` by\nconstruction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.hadamard_factorization_needs_nonneg_order","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.hadamard_factorization_needs_nonneg_order","kind":"theorem","line":268,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L268-L299","doc":"**The `0 ≤ ρ` hypothesis of `hadamard_factorization` is necessary** — without\nit the statement is FALSE (machine-proved, no `sorry`).\n\n`IsEntireOfOrderLE f ρ` is satisfiable at negative `ρ`: for `ρ < 0` the\nexponent `‖z‖^ρ` is bounded above on `‖z‖ ≥ 1` and Mathlib's `rpow` sends\n`0 ^ ρ` to `0`, so the constant function `1` satisfies it with `A = C = 1`.\nBut the conclusion demands `(p : ℝ) ≤ ρ` for a natural number `p`, and\n`(p : ℝ) ≥ 0 > ρ`. So the existential cannot be met.\n\nClassically the order of a nonzero entire function is `≥ 0` by construction,\nwhich is why the hypothesis is invisible in the textbooks; here the predicate\ntakes `ρ` as an input, so it has to be said. Same for `(g.natDegree : ℝ) ≤ ρ`.\nAxioms `[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.hadamard_factorization_statement_satisfiable_for_exp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.hadamard_factorization_statement_satisfiable_for_exp","kind":"theorem","line":254,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L254-L266","doc":"The repair is satisfiable exactly where the old statement failed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.no_nat_indexed_zero_enumeration_for_exp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.no_nat_indexed_zero_enumeration_for_exp","kind":"theorem","line":248,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L248-L252","doc":"The refutation: for `f = exp`, no `ℕ`-indexed zero enumeration exists. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.isEntireOfOrderLE_exp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.isEntireOfOrderLE_exp","kind":"theorem","line":242,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L242-L246","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.multipliable_canonicalProduct","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.multipliable_canonicalProduct","kind":"theorem","line":190,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L190-L223","doc":"**Canonical product convergence** (PI-3). If `∑ₙ ‖aₙ‖^{-(p+1)} < ∞` (i.e.\ngenus `≤ p`), the canonical product `∏ₙ Eₚ(z/aₙ)` converges (is multipliable)\nfor every `z`. Follows from `norm_one_sub_weierstrassFactor_le` plus the\nlog-summability criterion for infinite products. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.norm_one_sub_weierstrassFactor_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.norm_one_sub_weierstrassFactor_le","kind":"theorem","line":168,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L168-L188","doc":"**Weierstrass estimate** (convergence-critical, PI-2). For `‖z‖ ≤ 1`,\n`‖1 - Eₚ(z)‖ ≤ ‖z‖^{p+1}`. This is the bound that makes the canonical\nproduct converge.\n\nPROVED (2026-08-07), sorry-free, in `QOU.Mathlib.WeierstrassFactorEstimate`.\n\nNot by the textbook route. Ahlfors/Conway argue from the nonneg-coefficient\npower series `Eₚ(z) = 1 - ∑_{k>p} a_k z^k`, which needs real power-series\nmachinery. The proof used instead runs through the derivative identity\n`Eₚ'(z) = -z^p·exp(Sₚ(z))` — the telescoping `(1-z)∑_{k<p} z^k = 1 - z^p`\nkills the `-1` — giving `1 - Eₚ(z) = ∫₀¹ z^{p+1} t^p exp(Sₚ(tz)) dt`. The\nconstant is exactly `1` because that integral's real total mass is\n`1 - Eₚ(1) = 1` on the nose; the crude bound `exp(H_p)/(p+1)` exceeds `1`\nfor every `p ≥ 1` and would not suffice. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.IsGenus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.IsGenus","kind":"def","line":159,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L159-L164","doc":"`p` is the **genus** of the zero sequence `a`: the least natural number for\nwhich `∑ₙ ‖aₙ‖^{-(p+1)}` converges. Encoded as: the `(p+1)`-sum converges,\nand either `p = 0` or the `p`-sum diverges (minimality). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.IsEntireOfOrderLE","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.IsEntireOfOrderLE","kind":"def","line":151,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L151-L157","doc":"`f` is entire of **order of growth `≤ ρ`**: it is differentiable on all of\n`ℂ` and satisfies an exponential-type bound `‖f z‖ ≤ A · exp(C · ‖z‖^ρ)`.\nThe order of `f` is the infimum of such `ρ`; this predicate records the\nupper leg used by Hadamard's theorem (degree of `g` and genus `≤ ρ`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.differentiable_weierstrassFactor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.differentiable_weierstrassFactor","kind":"theorem","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L142-L147","doc":"Each Weierstrass primary factor is entire (a polynomial times the\nexponential of a polynomial). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.weierstrassFactor_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.weierstrassFactor_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L130-L140","doc":"A primary factor vanishes exactly at `z = 1` (the exponential never\nvanishes, so the only zero comes from the linear factor). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.weierstrassFactor_genus_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.weierstrassFactor_genus_zero","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L125-L128","doc":"Genus-zero primary factor is the bare linear factor `E₀(z) = 1 - z`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.weierstrassFactor_apply_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.weierstrassFactor_apply_zero","kind":"theorem","line":119,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L119-L123","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.weierstrassFactor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.HadamardFactorization.weierstrassFactor","kind":"def","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/HadamardFactorization.lean#L111-L117","doc":"The **Weierstrass primary factor** (elementary factor) of genus `p`:\n`Eₚ(z) = (1 - z)·exp(∑_{k=1}^{p} z^k / k)`.\nReindexed with `k = 0 … p-1` so the summand is `z^{k+1}/(k+1)`.\n`E₀(z) = 1 - z`; higher `p` add the exponential convergence-accelerating\nfactor that makes the canonical product converge. ","signatureChunk":0}]}